\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0 0 14px 0;\cf4 ">On March 19, 2026, the Trump administration announced the Federal Student Assistance Partnership &#8212; a multi-phase agreement transferring the $1.7 trillion federal student loan portfolio from the Department of Education to the Treasury Department. In the first phase, already underway, Treasury assumes responsibility for collecting on defaulted loans &#8212; approximately $180 billion affecting 9.2 million borrowers currently in default and 2.4 million in late-stage delinquency.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0 0 14px 0;\cf4 ">In subsequent phases, Treasury will assume responsibility for all non-defaulted loans and, eventually, administration of the FAFSA. Education Secretary Linda McMahon called it a "hard reset." Critics, including the American Federation of Government Employees and student loan advocacy groups, called the transfer "irresponsible and reckless," noting the Government Accountability Office has already warned that FSA staffing cuts compromised oversight of loan servicers.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0;\cf4 ">For current borrowers, the administration says no action is required &#8212; loans remain with the same servicers and are repaid the same way. The longer-term concern: as the Education Department is progressively dismantled, the policy expertise and legal authority protecting borrowers is dispersed across agencies without a history of student aid administration.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3     </\cf6 div\cf4 >\cb1 \
