\cf4 \cb3     <\cf6 div\cf4  style="\cf7 font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:bold;color:#1a2332;line-height:1.25;margin-bottom:6px;\cf4 ">The Pell Grant Is Running Out of Money: A $5.5B Shortfall and What It Means for College Access</\cf6 div\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3     <\cf6 div\cf4  style="\cf7 font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:14px;font-style:italic;font-weight:300;color:#3a4a5c;line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:14px;\cf4 ">CBO projects a $5.5 billion Pell Grant shortfall for FY 2026 alone, growing to $11.5 billion in FY 2027. Over 60 organizations are urging Congress to act before September 30. Seven million students face risk if nothing changes.</\cf6 div\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3     <\cf6 div\cf4  style="\cf7 height:2px;background-color:#c9963a;width:50px;margin-bottom:18px;\cf4 "></\cf6 div\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3     <\cf6 div\cf4  style="\cf7 font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:14px;color:#3d4f63;line-height:1.78;\cf4 ">\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0 0 14px 0;\cf4 ">The Pell Grant program &#8212; the cornerstone of federal college access for low-income students, providing up to $6,335 annually to more than seven million recipients &#8212; is facing a structural funding crisis. The Congressional Budget Office's February 2026 baseline projects a $5.5 billion shortfall for fiscal year 2026 and a cumulative gap of $104 billion over the next decade. Congress provided a one-time $10.5 billion injection last year, but it was insufficient to resolve the underlying structural mismatch.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0 0 14px 0;\cf4 ">The crisis stems from two intersecting forces: the FAFSA Simplification Act of 2020 expanded eligibility, bringing 1.7 million additional students into Pell qualification; and the Workforce Pell expansion taking effect July 1, 2026 extends grants to short-term training programs. More students qualifying is a policy success &#8212; but Congress has not funded the expansion at the levels required to sustain it.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3       <\cf6 p\cf4  style="\cf7 margin:0;\cf4 ">Over 60 organizations &#8212; including the NAACP, the American Federation of Teachers, and the Institute for College Access and Success &#8212; sent a letter to congressional leadership urging $16.5 billion in emergency funding. If Congress does not act before September 30, students could face eligibility restrictions or reduced awards beginning with the 2028&#8211;2029 academic year.</\cf6 p\cf4 >\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3     </\cf6 div\cf4 >\cb1 \
